Chapter: NumCheck
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Judge chapter Grade
  cites Gold chapter NumGold

 Grade Safari chapter Num -- rounding, flooring, modulo and the exponential --
 against zig's own builtins.

 THIS IS THE ONE CHECK WHOSE ORACLE IS NOT A GAME MODULE. Num is not a port of a
 wasm/ file; it is the Real primitives Codex's foreword does not have, written to
 zig's semantics because zig is what the game calls. So probe_num.zig imports
 nothing and the oracle is `@round`, `@floor`, `@mod`, `@exp` and `@exp2`
 themselves.

 AND IT IS THE ONE CHECK WITH NO f32/f64 GAP TO ABSORB. Every other seam compares
 a Real the port computed against an f32 the game computed, so a tolerance is
 forced on it. Here both sides are f64 and the operations are the same
 operations, so rounding, flooring, modulo and the power of two are graded at
 tolerance 0.0. Only `exp-real` gets a tolerance, because it is a Taylor series
 against a library function and the two are not the same algorithm.

 WHY THIS CHAPTER IS WORTH A CHECK AT ALL, given that ten chapters use it and all
 of them are graded: because those ten only ever call it on their own domains.
 Every `@round` in the game rounds a 0..255 colour channel, a stride count, a step
 count or a whole number of degrees, so a rounding that is wrong on a value none
 of them produces is invisible to every check in judge/ and is still a trap for
 the next caller. `exp-real` is the sharper case: its docstring claims a
 truncation remainder "around 1e-17" and nothing measured it. This port's own
 FINDINGS.md sends upstream a Cordic docstring that claims ~0.1% and delivers
 0.45%, with a test that never calls a Cordic function. An unmeasured accuracy
 claim in our own chapter is the same defect, and it should not survive being
 written down twice.

 THE INPUTS COME FROM THE GOLD, and that is a deliberate difference from the rest
 of judge/. CameraCheck and its probe each hold their own copy of the input table,
 written twice in two languages and kept in step by hand; the length check is the
 only thing guarding the pair. Here the probe prints its tables as `n-*-in` gold
 lines and this chapter maps over those, so there is exactly ONE copy of every
 input and drift between the arms is not a thing that can happen. It costs the
 tables their readability at this end, which is why probe_num.zig explains each
 one where it is written.

 We say:

Section: Mapping

 One shape per primitive: walk the gold's input list and apply the chapter under
 test. Nothing here is clever; the interest is entirely in the tables.

 THE DIVISOR IS THE OUTER LOOP, matching the probe's `for (MOD_M) |m| for (MOD_X)
 |x|`. Getting this the wrong way round would compare a correct set of answers in
 the wrong order, which the grader would report as a disagreement at index 13 --
 true, and the least useful true thing it could say.

  mod-inner : Real, List Real, Integer -> List Real
  mod-inner (m) (xs) (j) =
    if j >= list-length xs then []
    else [mod-real (list-at xs j) m] & mod-inner m xs (j + 1)

  mod-outer : List Real, List Real, Integer -> List Real
  mod-outer (ms) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ms then []
    else (mod-inner (list-at ms i) xs 0) & mod-outer ms xs (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "n-round" (list-map round-real g-n-round-in) g-n-round 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "n-floor" (list-map floor-real g-n-floor-in) g-n-floor 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "n-mod  " (mod-outer g-n-mod-m g-n-mod-x 0) g-n-mod 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "n-pow2 " (list-map pow2-int g-n-pow2-k) g-n-pow2 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel   "n-exp  " (list-map exp-real g-n-exp-in) g-n-exp 0.000000000000001)
  end
